1
-1:59:59,970 --> 00:00:04,860
On 20 April 1999, an ordinary school day in Littleton,

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,720
Colorado became a story
the whole country watched live.

3
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:12,713
Outside the building, police did not yet
know that the attack was already over.

4
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:18,493
This is the story of what the country saw,
how the response was reviewed, and what changed.

5
00:00:29,037 --> 00:00:33,847
Columbine High School is in Littleton,
a suburb of Denver, in Jefferson County, Colorado.

6
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:40,500
On Tuesday, 20 April 1999, an attack
began there in the late morning.

7
00:00:40,770 --> 00:00:44,460
Thirteen people were killed:
twelve students and one teacher.

8
00:00:44,903 --> 00:00:49,113
Others were wounded, and the school community
has been living with that day ever since.

9
00:00:49,803 --> 00:00:54,313
The state review commission later put the end
of the attack at about eight minutes past noon,

10
00:00:54,823 --> 00:00:56,593
less than an hour after it began.

11
00:00:57,203 --> 00:01:00,993
Television stations in Denver started
live coverage that same morning.

12
00:01:01,517 --> 00:01:03,977
Shooting at a high school in Littleton, Colorado.

13
00:01:03,997 --> 00:01:04,677
That's near Denver.

14
00:01:05,157 --> 00:01:06,517
Here is what we know so far.

15
00:01:06,537 --> 00:01:13,507
One station, KUSA, stayed live for about ten hours,
and CNN and NBC rebroadcast its pictures.

16
00:01:14,083 --> 00:01:17,983
It was the one place you used to think
that your child would be safe.

17
00:01:18,123 --> 00:01:22,043
Instead, our schools are turning into killing fields.

18
00:01:22,263 --> 00:01:26,683
It happened again today in Littleton,
Colorado, and this time it was a massacre.

19
00:01:26,703 --> 00:01:31,563
At one point the sheriff's department asked
stations to stop showing live helicopter pictures,

20
00:01:31,583 --> 00:01:34,753
because they could reveal where
SWAT teams were standing.

21
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:37,680
This video does not show those pictures.

22
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:42,320
They include people in distress,
and that is a line we will not cross.

23
00:01:43,037 --> 00:01:45,827
But the live pictures showed only part of that day.

24
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:50,887
The commission wrote that responders
outside did not know the attackers were dead,

25
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:54,047
so it took several more hours to secure the building.

26
00:01:54,800 --> 00:02:00,090
We have an unsafe situation due
to the amount of bombs we found in the school.

27
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:02,470
We removed 12 of them out of there so far.

28
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:08,770
We're running into booby traps at different
locations due to the late hour here right now.

29
00:02:08,790 --> 00:02:11,650
And we're going to secure the site for the night.

30
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:13,510
CBS News later reported

31
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:18,460
that a wounded teacher waited nearly four
hours before reaching the SWAT team.

32
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:22,327
To see why that happened, you have
to know how police were trained in 1999.

33
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:33,420
At 7:48 that evening, President Bill Clinton
spoke from the White House Briefing Room.

34
00:02:33,637 --> 00:02:35,327
Here is part of what he said.

35
00:02:35,883 --> 00:02:41,893
We don't know yet all the hows
and whys of this tragedy.

36
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:45,693
Perhaps we may never fully understand it.

37
00:02:45,903 --> 00:02:49,653
He also passed on something a local
official had said to him that night.

38
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:57,020
Perhaps now America would wake up
to the dimensions of this challenge

39
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,060
if it could happen in a place like Littleton

40
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:04,120
and we could prevent anything
like this from happening again.

41
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,460
We pray that she is right.

42
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:09,840
Then he spoke directly to the families.

43
00:03:10,617 --> 00:03:12,887
To the families who have lost their loved ones,

44
00:03:13,387 --> 00:03:17,467
to the parents who have lost their beloved children,

45
00:03:18,567 --> 00:03:21,227
to the wounded children and their families,

46
00:03:21,787 --> 00:03:24,247
to the people of the community of Littleton.

47
00:03:25,227 --> 00:03:30,207
I can only say tonight that the prayers
of the American people are with you.

48
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:33,840
Asked what should be done,
he said the facts had to come first.

49
00:03:34,617 --> 00:03:37,047
What I would like to do is take a couple of days

50
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:40,687
because we don't know what the facts are here.

51
00:03:42,087 --> 00:03:47,247
And keep in mind, the community
is an open wound right now.

52
00:03:47,267 --> 00:03:49,667
They have suffered as much as anyone can suffer.

53
00:03:50,003 --> 00:03:54,673
A month later, First Lady Hillary Clinton came
to Littleton to speak to the school community.

54
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:58,443
And to promise ourselves
that we will do everything we can

55
00:03:58,463 --> 00:04:01,183
to prevent it from happening anywhere else.

56
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:03,183
The memories of those who died

57
00:04:03,203 --> 00:04:06,103
will make us stronger in broken places.

58
00:04:06,203 --> 00:04:07,553
Those were words of comfort.

59
00:04:08,363 --> 00:04:10,193
The harder questions were about the response.

60
00:04:15,937 --> 00:04:22,827
In 1999, many police departments taught first officers
to set a perimeter and wait for specialist teams.

61
00:04:23,537 --> 00:04:27,427
The approach is sometimes
summarized as time, talk and tactics.

62
00:04:28,270 --> 00:04:34,040
The reasoning was caution: a trained team was
seen as safer than one or two officers going in alone.

63
00:04:34,537 --> 00:04:37,607
Columbine put that approach
under a very public test.

64
00:04:38,170 --> 00:04:41,600
The Jefferson County Sheriff's Office
led the criminal investigation,

65
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:46,440
and on 15 May 2000 it released
its final investigative report.

66
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:52,620
A judge had ordered that release by that date,
after families of victims asked to see the report.

67
00:04:53,037 --> 00:04:55,947
The report laid out the day with timelines, diagrams,

68
00:04:56,437 --> 00:05:00,047
photographs, and recordings
of radio traffic and 911 calls.

69
00:05:00,637 --> 00:05:04,067
The 911 tapes were released too, in July 2000.

70
00:05:04,737 --> 00:05:06,387
This video does not play them.

71
00:05:06,703 --> 00:05:11,003
Separately, Colorado Governor Bill Owens
had appointed a review commission,

72
00:05:11,023 --> 00:05:15,013
chaired by William Erickson,
a former state Supreme Court justice.

73
00:05:15,703 --> 00:05:20,613
The governor's charge to the commission was
to identify the lessons that Columbine taught.

74
00:05:21,037 --> 00:05:24,017
Its stated hope was that even one life could be saved

75
00:05:24,037 --> 00:05:28,447
by effective law enforcement and rescue
responses to a future emergency.

76
00:05:29,203 --> 00:05:32,003
Its central recommendation was that the first officers

77
00:05:32,023 --> 00:05:35,933
to arrive should make stopping
an ongoing attack their top priority.

78
00:05:36,437 --> 00:05:41,147
In plain words: do not wait for the specialists
if people are still in danger.

79
00:05:41,503 --> 00:05:43,413
Trainers call that rapid response.

80
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:48,440
The report explains why SWAT teams
act together: they train as teams,

81
00:05:48,970 --> 00:05:53,480
typically twenty hours a month, so that each
member knows what the others will do.

82
00:05:54,070 --> 00:05:58,240
The commission also stressed
that the situation was extremely chaotic.

83
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:04,460
Witness after witness told it that Columbine was
unlike any other tragedy they had ever experienced.

84
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:09,520
That training, it said, should reach every
officer who might be a first responder,

85
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:14,240
and every school resource officer,
whether or not they serve on a SWAT team.

86
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:18,020
It also asked for much more training
in incident command,

87
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:21,150
so that command officers can
take charge at the beginning

88
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:24,220
of a crisis and communicate their objectives clearly.

89
00:06:24,937 --> 00:06:27,207
The commission also looked beyond the police.

90
00:06:27,637 --> 00:06:31,607
It also asked for a threat assessment team
in every high school and middle school,

91
00:06:32,077 --> 00:06:34,847
and an anonymous reporting
line in every school district.

92
00:06:35,503 --> 00:06:40,273
The president had spoken of early warning
signs on the night of the attack, too.

93
00:06:40,817 --> 00:06:43,387
We must do more to reach out to our children

94
00:06:43,407 --> 00:06:46,247
and teach them to express their anger

95
00:06:46,267 --> 00:06:50,917
and to resolve their conflicts
with words, not weapons.

96
00:06:51,217 --> 00:06:52,847
And we do know we have to do more

97
00:06:52,867 --> 00:06:55,207
to recognize the early warning signs

98
00:06:55,227 --> 00:06:58,787
that are sent before children act violently.

99
00:06:58,937 --> 00:07:01,247
Not everyone was satisfied with the commission.

100
00:07:01,870 --> 00:07:06,060
A father of one of the students killed said
the report failed to hold authorities accountable.

101
00:07:11,537 --> 00:07:16,167
After Columbine, departments across the United
States adopted rapid response training.

102
00:07:16,637 --> 00:07:19,587
In 2002, Texas State University,

103
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:21,367
the San Marcos Police Department

104
00:07:21,717 --> 00:07:25,147
and the Hays County Sheriff's Office
formed a training center called ALERRT.

105
00:07:25,737 --> 00:07:28,727
A change in training is not the same as a result.

106
00:07:29,537 --> 00:07:32,327
This video does not claim that schools became safer.

107
00:07:32,937 --> 00:07:37,247
His advice to communities that night was
to study what lessons could be learned.

108
00:07:37,817 --> 00:07:40,967
But that shouldn't make people believe

109
00:07:40,987 --> 00:07:42,687
that every school is in danger.

110
00:07:42,707 --> 00:07:44,607
What it should make every community do

111
00:07:44,627 --> 00:07:48,267
is to study this handbook we put out

112
00:07:48,287 --> 00:07:51,447
and see what lessons can be learned here.

113
00:07:51,570 --> 00:07:56,260
The Columbine Memorial opened
to the public on 21 September 2007.

114
00:07:56,903 --> 00:07:59,133
At its center is the Ring of Remembrance,

115
00:07:59,503 --> 00:08:02,413
carrying words about the thirteen
written by their own families.

116
00:08:02,937 --> 00:08:06,487
The pictures of that day belong to the families
and the people who were there.

117
00:08:07,137 --> 00:08:09,127
We have used the record of what came after.

118
00:08:10,303 --> 00:08:14,953
Thirteen people were lost that day,
and police training changed because of it.

119
00:08:16,037 --> 00:08:18,927
So are our schools safer
than they were that morning?

